== 6.9 Chapter 6 Practice Drill — 40 Questions

40 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 44 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [A literary device is best defined as],
  [a figure of speech],
  [a technique that changes what the reader must do],
  [an ornament added to plain writing],
  [a mark of good style]
)

#mcq(2, [Which family does personification belong to?],
  [sound],
  [statement],
  [structure],
  [comparison]
)

#mcq(3, [Hyperbole belongs to the family of],
  [sound],
  [comparison],
  [structure],
  [statement]
)

#mcq(4, [A comparison using *like* or *as* is],
  [an apostrophe],
  [a simile],
  [a metaphor],
  [a conceit]
)

#mcq(5, [“The city is a stone lung” is],
  [personification],
  [metonymy],
  [a metaphor],
  [a simile]
)

#mcq(6, [Giving human feelings to wind or weather is],
  [synecdoche],
  [personification or pathetic fallacy],
  [pun],
  [litotes]
)

#mcq(7, [“O Death, be not proud” is an example of],
  [apostrophe],
  [innuendo],
  [simile],
  [hyperbole]
)

#mcq(8, [“The crown has not spoken” uses],
  [personification],
  [synecdoche],
  [irony],
  [metonymy]
)

#mcq(9, [“All hands on deck” uses],
  [conceit],
  [metonymy],
  [euphemism],
  [synecdoche]
)

#mcq(10, [The test for telling metonymy from synecdoche is whether],
  [the line rhymes],
  [the comparison is stated],
  [the substitute is a part of the thing it replaces],
  [the term is a single word]
)

#mcq(11, [An elaborate comparison sustained through a whole poem is],
  [an image],
  [a simile],
  [a symbol],
  [a conceit]
)

#mcq(12, [Deliberate exaggeration for effect is],
  [litotes],
  [bathos],
  [hyperbole],
  [paradox]
)

#mcq(13, [“She was not displeased” is an example of],
  [irony],
  [euphemism],
  [hyperbole],
  [litotes]
)

#mcq(14, [A statement that seems self-contradictory and is nevertheless true is],
  [a cliché],
  [a paradox],
  [a pun],
  [an oxymoron]
)

#mcq(15, [“Bitter sweet” is],
  [a pun],
  [a paradox],
  [a litotes],
  [an oxymoron]
)

#mcq(16, [A mild term substituted for a harsh one is],
  [innuendo],
  [euphemism],
  [sarcasm],
  [litotes]
)

#mcq(17, [Irony differs from a lie because irony],
  [is spoken aloud],
  [always wounds],
  [expects the hearer to recover the intended meaning],
  [uses exaggeration]
)

#mcq(18, [Sarcasm is best described as],
  [a contradictory phrase],
  [an extended comparison],
  [irony with a sting, aimed at a person],
  [gentle understatement]
)

#mcq(19, [A pun works by],
  [repeating a consonant],
  [exaggerating a claim],
  [reversing the speaker's meaning],
  [playing on two meanings of one word]
)

#mcq(20, [Satire is distinguished from mere mockery by],
  [its comedy],
  [its use of irony],
  [a corrective aim, judged by a standard the satire implies],
  [its length]
)

#mcq(21, [Alliteration repeats],
  [initial consonants],
  [vowel sounds],
  [final consonants],
  [whole phrases]
)

#mcq(22, [Assonance is the repetition of],
  [vowel sounds],
  [initial consonants],
  [stress patterns],
  [final consonants]
)

#mcq(23, [Consonance is the repetition of],
  [initial consonants],
  [vowel sounds],
  [rhyme endings],
  [final consonants]
)

#mcq(24, [*The thick thorns thrust through the thatch* is dominated by],
  [onomatopoeia],
  [alliteration],
  [assonance],
  [litotes]
)

#mcq(25, [Onomatopoeia occurs when],
  [the line ends with a rhyme],
  [the word imitates the sound it names],
  [two ideas are balanced],
  [an object is addressed]
)

#mcq(26, [Parallelism in a sentence],
  [uses two contradictory words],
  [exaggerates],
  [repeats a grammatical pattern],
  [reverses the word order]
)

#mcq(27, [Antithesis works by],
  [placing opposed ideas in balanced clauses],
  [repeating a whole line],
  [imitating a sound],
  [addressing the dead]
)

#mcq(28, [A refrain is],
  [a grammatical pattern],
  [a silent pause],
  [a repeated line or phrase at intervals],
  [a rhyme inside a line]
)

#mcq(29, [Climax as a device arranges material so that],
  [the first item is strongest],
  [the items are equal],
  [nothing is resolved],
  [the items rise in force and the last lands hardest]
)

#mcq(30, [Anticlimax differs from bathos in that anticlimax],
  [is a structural let-down, while bathos is a collapse inside the writing],
  [belongs only to drama],
  [is always comic],
  [requires a rhyme]
)

#mcq(31, [Juxtaposition works by],
  [repeating a phrase],
  [addressing an object],
  [placing two things side by side so that each measures the other],
  [exaggerating]
)

#mcq(32, [Foreshadowing is],
  [a scene from the past],
  [a contradiction],
  [a repeated line],
  [a hint that will matter later]
)

#mcq(33, [A transferred epithet occurs in],
  [“all hands on deck”],
  [“not unkind”],
  [“he lay in a sleepless bed”],
  [“the wind wept”]
)

#mcq(34, [Zoomorphism is],
  [exaggerating an animal],
  [giving animals human traits],
  [describing a human in animal terms],
  [addressing an object]
)

#mcq(35, [“There is a metaphor in line 3.” This answer is weak because it],
  [quotes the wrong line],
  [names the device without giving its effect],
  [is factually wrong],
  [uses a technical term]
)

#mcq(36, [The first question to ask of an unseen extract is],
  [what genre is it, and what may I therefore quote],
  [who wrote it],
  [what is the theme],
  [how long is it]
)

#mcq(37, [An answer that paraphrases the extract without naming a device is],
  [an effect],
  [comprehension, not appreciation],
  [an evaluation],
  [a full answer]
)

#mcq(38, [“The writer uses imagery to create a picture.” The weakness is that it],
  [does not say which sense or what the reader now understands],
  [is too long],
  [names the wrong device],
  [uses the passive voice]
)

#mcq(39, [Inventing a device that is not in the text is worse than describing the effect plainly because],
  [a wrong name loses the mark a correct description would have kept],
  [it is longer],
  [it is dishonest],
  [examiners dislike technical terms]
)

#mcq(40, [The best single sentence shape for an effect is],
  [“The writer uses X.”],
  [“X appears in line 2.”],
  [“X is a device.”],
  [“X makes the reader …”]
)

