  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The device does work — comparing, sounding, withholding — and the work is the effect. Ornament is the word that keeps candidates at the level of naming.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Personification transfers human qualities onto something else, which is a comparison across two categories.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Exaggeration manages the distance between what is said and what is meant, which is the statement family.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The explicit comparative word marks the simile. A metaphor asserts identity without it, and a conceit is an extended comparison.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It asserts identity — the city *is* a lung — without *like* or *as*, which is the metaphor's form.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Personification gives human qualities to the non-human; pathetic fallacy makes nature share the mood of the scene.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Death is addressed directly as though present and able to hear, which is apostrophe.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The crown is associated with the monarch without being a part of him: metonymy.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hands are part of the crew, so the part stands for the whole: synecdoche.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Part-for-whole is synecdoche; association without parthood is metonymy.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The conceit is developed and tested across the poem, often to a strained or surprising point.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hyperbole overstates; litotes understates by denying the opposite.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Understatement by negating the opposite is litotes, and its effect is restraint rather than weakness.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The contradiction lives in the whole statement; an oxymoron puts it inside two words.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two contradictory words joined in a phrase make an oxymoron.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Euphemism softens — *passed on* for *died*. Innuendo suggests without stating.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The ironic speaker relies on the audience hearing the reversal. A lie relies on them missing it.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sarcasm is aimed and wounding; irony can be structural, dramatic or entirely unmalicious.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Both senses must be live at once for the pun to work, which is why it is often used for comedy and for bitter wit.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Satire wants the target changed, and it therefore implies a standard — which is what a question about satire usually asks you to identify.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Initial consonants. Assonance repeats vowels, consonance repeats final consonants.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Vowel repetition gives a line its hum and often slows it.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Final consonants — the hard or soft edge of a line, as in *stroke / luck*.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The repeated initial *th* is alliteration, and its friction makes the line laborious.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    *Hiss*, *clang* and *rustle* carry their sound in their letters.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Parallel grammar makes two ideas weigh the same, which is why it is the favourite device of argument.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The balance makes the contrast feel inevitable, as in *they gave me a flag and a name, and a grave that was not mine*.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The repetition turns the poem ritualistic, and the change between appearances carries the argument.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ascending force is the structure; anticlimax reverses it deliberately.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One arranges events, the other collapses the style; both deflate, but only bathos is a joke about the prose itself.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The meaning is produced by the adjacency, not by either element alone.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It plants knowledge the reader cannot yet use, and suspense is what grows from it.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The adjective *sleepless* belongs to the man and is moved onto the bed.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is the reverse of personification: *he barked his orders* makes a man a dog.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Naming is only one part of the three-part answer; the mark for effect is still unearned.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Genre decides the evidence: action, narration or compression. Theme comes later and depends on it.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Paraphrase shows you understood the words; appreciation shows what the words do.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A picture is not an effect. The answer needs the specific image and the understanding it produces.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A plain description can still earn the effect mark; a confident misnaming removes the foundation of the answer.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    “It makes the reader …” forces the answer to name a consequence, which is what the mark scheme rewards.
  ]

]

